7. DESCRIPTION OF MAIN CLASSES
7.1 Vehicle Class

The Vehicle class is the base class for vehicles.

It stores:

Vehicle ID
Brand
Model
Rent per day
Availability status

It provides the basic properties and operations required for vehicles.

7.2 Car Class

The Car class extends the Vehicle class.

In addition to the properties inherited from Vehicle, it stores the number of seats.

This class demonstrates the concept of inheritance.

7.3 Bike Class

The Bike class also extends the Vehicle class.

It adds engine capacity in CC as an additional property.

This allows bikes to have their own specific information while sharing common vehicle properties.

7.4 Customer Class

The Customer class stores information about customers.

It contains:

Customer ID
Customer name
Phone number

This class is used to maintain customer records.

7.5 Rental Class

The Rental class connects a customer with a vehicle.

It stores the rental duration and calculates the total rental bill.

It demonstrates object composition because it contains references to both Customer and Vehicle.

7.6 Main Class

The Main class controls the overall application flow.

It contains:

ArrayLists
Menu system
Vehicle operations
Customer operations
Rental operations
Searching
Vehicle removal
File handling

It acts as the main entry point of the application.

8. OOP CONCEPTS USED
Encapsulation

Encapsulation means grouping data and related operations inside classes.

In this project, vehicle, customer, and rental information is organized into separate classes.

Inheritance

Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and methods from another class.

In this project:
Vehicle
   ├── Car
   └── Bike
Both Car and Bike extend the Vehicle class.

Polymorphism

Polymorphism allows a Vehicle reference to refer to different types of vehicles such as Car and Bike.

The overridden displayVehicle() methods can be invoked at runtime depending on the actual object type.

Composition

Composition is used in the Rental class.

A rental contains references to both a Customer and a Vehicle.

These concepts are specifically demonstrated in the project implementation.

9. ARRAYLIST / COLLECTIONS

The project uses ArrayList to store collections of vehicles, customers, and rentals.

ArrayList allows the application to dynamically store and manage multiple objects.

It makes operations such as adding, searching, displaying, and removing objects easier to implement.

10. FILE HANDLING

The application uses text files for storing data.

The three files used are:
vehicles.txt
customers.txt
rentals.txtVehicle information is stored in vehicles.txt, customer information is stored in customers.txt, and rental information is stored in rentals.txt.

The application loads data when it starts and provides an option to save data through the application.

